# PocketBase Integration Guide for Next.js

Since I could not locate the Next.js project root in your current workspace, I have prepared the specific code files you need to create.

## 1. Environment Variables

Create or update `.env.local` in your Next.js project root:

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_POCKETBASE_URL=https://db.vinod.app
# OR if running locally:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POCKETBASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8090
```

## 2. PocketBase Client (`lib/pocketbase.ts`)

Create this file to initialize the PocketBase client (Singleton pattern).

```typescript
// lib/pocketbase.ts
import PocketBase from 'pocketbase';

const url = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_POCKETBASE_URL || 'http://127.0.0.1:8090';
export const pb = new PocketBase(url);
```

## 3. Tick Buffering Hook (`hooks/useTickBuffer.ts`)

This hook implements the logic: "batch tick updates and send them to PocketBase every 5 seconds or when the buffer reaches 50 ticks".

```typescript
// hooks/useTickBuffer.ts
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { pb } from '../lib/pocketbase';

interface TickData {
  instrument_token: number;
  last_price: number;
  [key: string]: any;
}

export function useTickBuffer() {
  const bufferRef = useRef<TickData[]>([]);
  const timerRef = useRef<NodeJS.Timeout | null>(null);

  const flushBuffer = async () => {
    if (bufferRef.current.length === 0) return;

    const batch = [...bufferRef.current];
    bufferRef.current = []; // Clear buffer immediately
    
    try {
      // PocketBase helper to batch create is not native in JS SDK for single collection 
      // in one request effectively without a loop or custom endpoint, 
      // but we can loop or use a custom API endpoint if available.
      // Based on standard usage, we loop or use Promise.all.
      // For better performance, consider a custom PocketBase API route.
      
      // Attempting parallel creation (Caution: Rate limits)
      const promises = batch.map(tick => {
        return pb.collection('ticks').create({
          instrument_token: tick.instrument_token,
          last_price: tick.last_price,
          volume: tick.volume || 0,
          timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
          raw_data: tick
        }).catch(err => console.error("Failed to save tick:", err));
      });
      
      await Promise.all(promises);
      console.log(`Flushed ${batch.length} ticks to PocketBase.`);
      
    } catch (err) {
      console.error("Error flushing ticks:", err);
    }
  };

  const addTick = (tick: TickData) => {
    bufferRef.current.push(tick);

    // Check size limit
    if (bufferRef.current.length >= 50) {
        if (timerRef.current) clearTimeout(timerRef.current);
        flushBuffer();
        // Restart timer
        timerRef.current = setInterval(flushBuffer, 5000);
    }
  };

  useEffect(() => {
    // Start timer
    timerRef.current = setInterval(flushBuffer, 5000);

    return () => {
      if (timerRef.current) clearInterval(timerRef.current);
      flushBuffer(); // Flush remaining on unmount
    };
  }, []);

  return { addTick };
}
```

## 4. Usage in your Component

Integrate the hook where you receive WebSocket messages (e.g., in your Grid or Dashboard component).

```typescript
import { useTickBuffer } from '../hooks/useTickBuffer';

// ... inside your component
const { addTick } = useTickBuffer();

// Inside your WebSocket onmessage handler:
ws.onmessage = (event) => {
    const tick = JSON.parse(event.data);
    
    // Update local state for UI
    // ... 
    
    // Send to buffer
    addTick(tick);
};
```

## 5. Security Note regarding Public Access

As per your instructions, ensure the `ticks` collection in PocketBase > Settings > API Rules > content of "Create Rule" is set to valid configuration (empty for public, or authenticated).
